# MobilePay payments

Learn about MobilePay, a popular payment method in Denmark and Finland.

MobilePay is a single-use card wallet payment method used in Denmark and Finland. It allows customers to authenticate and approve payments using the MobilePay app. When a customer pays with MobilePay, Stripe processes the underlying card transaction using card data received from MobilePay.
Payment method family: Wallets
Usability: Single-use
Access type: Instant access
Payment confirmation timing: Immediate
Settlement timing: Standard payout schedule
Pricing: https://stripe.com/en-us/pricing/local-payment-methods#mobilepay
## Eligibility and availability 

### Account eligibility
Business location: AT, BE, BG, CY, CZ, DE, DK, EE, ES, FI, FR, GR, HR, HU, IE, IT, LI, LT, LU, LV, MT, NL, NO, PL, PT, RO, SE, SI, SK
Account type: ✓ Merchant, ✓ Platform or marketplace (Connect)
Business model: ✓ B2B, ✓ B2C
Business category: View list of prohibited and restricted business categories
### Unsupported businesses

In addition to the industry and business categories listed in [Prohibited and restricted businesses](https://stripe.com/restricted-businesses), MobilePay prohibits the following categories:

- Bonds
- Debt collection
- Multi-level marketing and pyramid schemes

Review the list of *merchant category codes* (A Merchant Category Code (MCC) is a four-digit number that classifies the type of goods or services a business offers) that are prohibited or restricted for MobilePay.

- **Prohibited**: You can’t use this payment method with these MCCs.
- **Restricted**: You might be able to use this payment method with these MCCs, but you must provide additional information when you create the account.

| MCC | Description | Type |
| --- | --- | --- |
| 4829 | Money Orders - Wire Transfers | Prohibited |
| 6050 | Description unavailable | Prohibited |
| 6051 | Cryptocurrency exchanges and wallets | Prohibited |
| 6211 | Security Brokers and Dealers | Prohibited |
| 6529–6530 | Description unavailable | Prohibited |
| 6534 | Description unavailable | Prohibited |
| 7321 | Credit Reporting Agencies | Prohibited |
| 7801 | Government Licensed On-line Casinos (On-Line Gambling) | Prohibited |
| 7995 | Betting and Casino Gambling | Prohibited |
| 9222 | Fines - Government Administrative Entities | Prohibited |
| 9223 | Bail and Bond Payments | Prohibited |
| 9754 | Gambling - Horse and Dog Racing, State Lottery | Prohibited |

Stripe accounts in the following countries can accept MobilePay payments that settle in a supported currency.

EMEA: AT, BE, BG, CY, CZ, DE, DK, EE, ES, FI, FR, GR, HR, HU, IE, IT, LI, LT, LU, LV, MT, NL, NO, PL, PT, RO, SE, SI, SK

Customers in the following countries can use MobilePay.

EMEA: DK, FI

### Payment support
Customer location: DK, FI
Presentment currency: DKK, EUR, NOK, SEK
Geographic coverage: ✓ Domestic, ✓ Crossborder
Transaction limits: Minimum amount depends on the settlement currency.
The minimum charge amount depends on your payout bank account’s settlement currency. For example, payments that settle in DKK must be at least 2.50 DKK. If Stripe converts the payment, the converted amount must meet the settlement currency’s minimum. See [minimum and maximum charge amounts](https://docs.stripe.com/currencies.md#minimum-and-maximum-charge-amounts).

## Capabilities 
Recurring payments: ✗ Not supported

Payment authorizations:
  ✗ Extended authorizations
  ✗ Flexible extended authorizations
  ✗ Incremental authorizations
  ✗ Decremental authorizations
  ✗ Re-authorizations

Payment captures:
  ✓ Manual capture
  ✓ Partial capture
  ✗ Multi-capture
  ✗ Over-capture

Refunds:
  ✓ Partial refunds
  ✓ Full refunds
  Processing time: Typically 5–10 business days, depending on the issuer

Disputes:
  ✗ Partial disputes
  ✓ Full disputes
### Capture behavior 

MobilePay supports manual and partial capture. A partial capture is final: Stripe releases the uncaptured remainder, and you can’t make a second capture for it.

### Refunds 

MobilePay doesn’t impose a single payment-method-specific refund deadline. Standard card-network and regional rules determine how long after a payment you can submit a refund. You can issue full or partial refunds through the [Dashboard](https://dashboard.stripe.com/payments) or [Refunds API](https://docs.stripe.com/api/refunds/create.md), and you can issue multiple partial refunds provided that their total doesn’t exceed the original payment amount. Funds typically appear within 5–10 business days, depending on the issuer. Shortly after creation, some card refunds can be canceled when the Dashboard offers the **Cancel refund** action.

#### Refund process 

MobilePay refunds are processed through the card used for the original payment. Stripe submits the refund to the card issuer. A refund issued shortly after payment can appear as a reversal, where the original charge disappears rather than a separate credit appearing.

#### Tracking refunds 

Track refunds in the Dashboard or retrieve the [Refund object](https://docs.stripe.com/api/refunds/retrieve.md) through the API. Listen for `refund.updated` and `refund.failed`. [Verify webhook signatures](https://docs.stripe.com/webhooks.md#verify-events) to confirm that events came from Stripe. If you use IP allowlisting, allow Stripe’s [public IP addresses](https://docs.stripe.com/ips.md). Allow approximately 5–10 business days for a credit to appear, depending on the issuer. If the refund fails, inspect its failure reason and arrange another refund method where necessary.

### Disputes 

#### Dispute process 

MobilePay payments are processed using Visa or Mastercard card transactions. Customers can dispute the underlying card payment with their card issuer. There is no separate MobilePay-specific dispute workflow.

#### Dispute notifications 

Stripe notifies you of a dispute by email, in the Dashboard, and through a `charge.dispute.created` webhook event. The dispute reason and response deadline are available in the Dashboard and on the Dispute object.

#### Responding to disputes 

Respond and submit evidence through Stripe’s [standard card-dispute process](https://docs.stripe.com/disputes/responding.md). The applicable response deadline appears in the Dashboard and in `evidence_details.due_by` on the Dispute object.

#### Dispute outcomes 

The card issuer reviews the evidence and decides the outcome. If you win, Stripe returns the disputed amount to your balance. If you lose, the balance debit becomes permanent.

